        {
          "id": "49c2827a-e721-4f89-802b-224d26457aa9",
          "type": "column",
          "children": [
            {
              "id": "353d6032-55c8-4d20-ad00-1650270d870d",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "html": "The<strong> leisure class</strong>, a term popularized by American economist and sociologist Thorstein Veblen in his 1899 book <em><a href=\"#/the-theory-of-the-leisure-class\">The Theory of the Leisure Class</a></em>, refers to a social class that primarily engages in non-productive activities, such as <a href=\"#/leisure\">leisure</a>, recreation, and consumption. Veblen argued that the leisure <a href=\"#/class\">class</a> arose as a result of an accumulation of wealth and social status, which enabled its members to participate in conspicuous consumption and engage in activities that demonstrated their <a href=\"#/economics-d57cb758\">economic</a> and social power. In the context of play, the leisure class has been a driving force behind the development and popularization of various forms of games, <a href=\"#/sport\">sports</a>, and recreational activities throughout history, as they were often the primary or only audiences for these things."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "469d9408-8c2b-4246-93b2-e4278491bf3e",
          "type": "column",
          "children": [
            {
              "id": "3499b993-c7be-4aa3-90b5-22f81090912d",
              "type": "image",
              "src": "./assets/images/untitled-edaa65c9f1ea.webp",
              "caption": "<em>Lawn Tennis, </em>L. Prang &amp; Company, 1887",
              "alt": "Untitled"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "dda740d4-9a06-4d05-bc00-55c0369d1081",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "html": "The relationship between the leisure class and the world of games and play is complex and multifaceted. Members of the leisure class have often been patrons or early adopters of new forms of entertainment, helping to shape the cultural landscape and influence the development of games, sports, and leisure activities. Furthermore, the leisure class has historically used games and play as a means of establishing and reinforcing social hierarchies, as well as a way to cultivate social networks and alliances. The concept of the leisure class provides valuable insights into the social and cultural dimensions of play, highlighting the ways in which games can both reflect and shape societal values and structures."
    },
